.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt IOPRIO_SET 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ioprio_set ,
.Nm ioprio_get
.Nd set or get the I/O priority of threads
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_ioprio_set "int which" "int who" "int ioprio"
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_ioprio_get "int which" "int who"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Each thread has an I/O priority, its class shifted left 13 bits and its
level, as on Linux: class 0, none; 1, real time; 2, best effort; 3, idle.
A thread starts with class none and level 0;
.Xr fork 2
and
.Xr clone 2
give the new thread the caller's, and
.Xr execve 2
keeps it.
No disk queue reads it: every request is served in the order it came,
whatever its thread's priority.
util-linux's
.Ic ionice
sets and shows it.
.Pp
.Fa which
and
.Fa who
select the threads as
.Xr getpriority 2
does, with numbers one greater:
.Dv IOPRIO_WHO_PROCESS ,
1, the thread whose ID is
.Fa who ;
.Dv IOPRIO_WHO_PGRP ,
2, every thread of the process group
.Fa who ;
.Dv IOPRIO_WHO_USER ,
3, every thread whose real user ID is
.Fa who .
A
.Fa who
of 0 selects the calling thread, its process group, or its real user ID.
.Pp
.Fn ioprio_set
gives each selected thread
.Fa ioprio .
A level runs from 0 to 7, class none takes only level 0, and the idle
class ignores its level.
The real-time class needs a caller with effective user ID 0.
A caller with effective user ID 0 may change any thread; any other only a
thread whose real user ID is the caller's real or effective user ID.
The threads are changed in turn until one may not be, as on Linux, and
the call then fails with that refusal.
.Pp
.Fn ioprio_get
returns the priority of the one thread selected, or the best of those
selected: the lowest value, with a thread of class none compared as best
effort, level 4, as Linux compares it.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 289 for
.Fn ioprio_set
and 290 for
.Fn ioprio_get .
EBX contains
.Fa which ,
ECX
.Fa who ,
and EDX
.Fa ioprio .
On x86-64 the numbers are 251 and 252, with the same arguments in RDI, RSI
and RDX.
musl and glibc have no wrapper of their own.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn ioprio_set
returns 0, and
.Fn ioprio_get
the priority.
On failure both return -1 and set
.Va errno .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/musl/schedmusl.c
sets and reads the priority of the process, checks that a child inherits
it, and that a caller of another user ID may take the idle class but not
the real-time one, nor change root's process.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa which
is none of the three, or
.Fa ioprio
has an unknown class, a level past 7, or class none with a level.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The real-time class from a caller without effective user ID 0, or a
thread whose real user ID is neither the caller's real nor its effective
user ID.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
No thread was selected.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getpriority 2 ,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2
